\subsection{Attempt: fewer requests versus faster human contact}
I tested one operational meaning of distance: time until a request receives a completed response. Consider a first-come-first-served queue with Poisson request rate $\lambda>0$, independent exponential service rate $\mu>\lambda$, and a stationary backlog. This is a standard queueing model introduced for illustration, not an asserted description of instructor relationships.
\begin{proposition}
A request arriving in this stationary model has expected time in the system $1/(\mu-\lambda)$. Reducing the request rate can nevertheless increase that time if service capacity decreases more.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Put $\rho=\lambda/\mu<1$. The probabilities $\pi_n=(1-\rho)\rho^n$ sum to one and satisfy $\lambda\pi_n=\mu\pi_{n+1}$ for every $n\ge0$. These adjacent balances give zero net flow at each state of the birth-and-death queue and hence a stationary distribution. The total jump rates are bounded by $\lambda+\mu$; equivalently, one can use a Poisson clock of that rate and a discrete transition matrix. The same balances preserve $\pi$ at every clock step, proving stationarity directly.

The rate of arrivals seeing pre-arrival backlog $n$ is $\lambda\pi_n$: conditional on the past, the independent Poisson arrival probability in a small interval of length $h$ is $\lambda h+O(h^2)$, and an additional queue jump has probability $O(h)$, so simultaneous changes affect the count only by $O(h^2)$. Dividing the state-specific arrival rate by total rate $\lambda$ gives arrival-state probabilities $\pi_n$. Conditional on seeing $n$ requests present, the tagged request waits for $n+1$ exponential service completions, each of mean $1/\mu$, using memorylessness for the current service. Its mean time is therefore
\[
\frac1\mu\sum_{n\ge0}(n+1)(1-\rho)\rho^n
=\frac1{\mu(1-\rho)}=\frac1{\mu-\lambda}.
\]
The geometric-series identity follows by summing $\sum_{j\ge0}\sum_{n\ge j}(1-\rho)\rho^n=\sum_{j\ge0}\rho^j$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
Rates $(\lambda,\mu)=(8,10)$ give mean time $1/2$, while $(6,7)$ give mean time one. Thus fewer requests alone do not certify faster responses. The Poisson, service, stationarity and independence assumptions are essential; non-Poisson sampling does not generally see the time-average backlog. This calculation does not equate response delay with relational or epistemic distance and does not evaluate any actual technology. The source's broader notion of distance still needs definition and data.

